<%# @games, not a fresh Game.by(current_user). This partial used to build its
    own copy of the same scope, which threw away the includes(:runs) the
    controller had just paid for -- so the games table read game.starts_at,
    game.max_team_number and game.status off unpreloaded records and fetched
    one game's runs at a time. Same rows either way: DashboardController#index
    assigns @games from that identical scope.
    size, not count: @games is already loaded here, and count would send a
    COUNT for a number Ruby is holding. %>
<%# card--wide because this is the one dashboard card holding a table. Six
    columns need 800px; half of the grid gave it 518 and .table-wrap scrolled
    the difference sideways, with every date wrapped onto three lines. Full
    width it needs 1086 and gets 1086. See .dash-grid in screens.css.
    The id stays: features/games/hide-draft-game.feature says
    `в блоке "mygames"` and that file is frozen. %>
<fieldset id="mygames" class="card card--wide">
  <legend>
    <%= t("dashboard.my_games.legend", count: @games.size) %>
  </legend>

  <%= render "games/list", games: @games %>

  <p>
    <%= link_to t("dashboard.my_games.create"), new_game_path, class: "btn" %>
  </p>
</fieldset>
